From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 05:25:50 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 2/8] Serve peers that register over TCP/WebSocket, and relay
 WebRTC signaling

A controlled peer behind a firewall that only lets HTTPS out cannot reach
the UDP port, so the client registers over TCP/WebSocket instead (start_tcp
with allow-websocket or a proxy): RegisterPk on a kept connection, then
heartbeats it echoes back. hbbs answered that with NOT_SUPPORT and only
pushed punch hole and relay requests over UDP, so such a peer never came
online. Now:

- RegisterPk over TCP/WebSocket registers the peer like over UDP and makes
  the connection its signaling channel; PunchHole, RequestRelay and
  IceCandidate for it go through that connection. It gets an empty frame
  every 10s, echoing it refreshes its online state, and closing it takes
  the peer offline at once.
- Every TCP/WebSocket connection is written by its own task through a
  queue, so it stays addressable after a reply: a WebRTC controller keeps
  receiving ICE candidates after the answer.
- PunchHole carries the controller's webrtc_sdp_offer and force_relay,
  PunchHoleResponse the peer's webrtc_sdp_answer, and IceCandidate is routed
  both ways (by peer id to the peer, by socket_addr to the controller).
- OnlineRequest is answered on the WebSocket endpoint, where clients in
  WebSocket mode send it.
- X-Real-Port from the reverse proxy completes X-Real-IP: connections are
  keyed by address, and clients behind one NAT all collided on ip:0.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
